  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 2,
   "text": "Discriminants"
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 3,
   "text": "What is a discriminant?"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- The discriminant is the part of the quadratic formula that is under the square root sign $b^{2}-4ac$\n- It is sometimes denoted by the Greek letter $Δ$ (capital delta)"
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 3,
   "text": "How does the discriminant relate to graphs and roots?"
  },
  {
   "type": "paragraph",
   "md": "There are three options for the outcome of the discriminant:"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- If $b^{2}-4ac>0$ the quadratic crosses the x-axis **twice **meaning there are **two distinct real roots**\n- If  $b^{2}-4ac=0$ the quadratic touches the x-axis **once **meaning there is **one real root** (also called repeated roots)\n- If  $b^{2}-4ac<0$ the quadratic **does not cross** the x-axis meaning there are no real roots"
  },
  {
   "type": "figure",
   "orig_src": "https://cdn.savemyexams.com/uploads/2020/06/2.2.2-Discrimamts-Notes-Diagram-2.png",
   "file": "assets/74e5af0fc89b-2-2-2-discrimamts-notes-diagram-2.png",
   "alt": "Discrimamts Notes Diagram 2, A Level & AS Level Pure Maths Revision Notes",
   "caption": null
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 3,
   "text": "Discriminant and inequalities"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- You need to be able to **set** **up** and **solve** equations and inequalities (often quadratic) arising from the discriminant\n- Sketch the quadratic and decide whether you're looking above or below zero to write your solutions correctly"
  },
  {
   "type": "figure",
   "orig_src": "https://cdn.savemyexams.com/uploads/2020/06/2.2.2-Discrimamts-Notes-Diagram-3.png",
   "file": "assets/c699ed2b041f-2-2-2-discrimamts-notes-diagram-3.png",
   "alt": "Discrimamts Notes Diagram 3, A Level & AS Level Pure Maths Revision Notes",
   "caption": null
  },
  {
   "type": "callout",
   "variant": "exam-tip",
   "label": "Exam Hint",
   "md": "- When questions just mention “real roots”, the roots could be **distinct** or **repeated** (i.e. they aren't talking about complex numbers!)\n- In these cases, you only need to worry about solving $b^{2}-4ac\\geq0$\n- When solving using inequalities, always sketch the quadratic and decide whether you're looking above or below zero to help write your solutions correctly"
  },
